def lps(s):
    n = len(s)
    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]

    # Fill the DP table
    for i in range(2, n + 1):
        for j in range(1, n - i + 2):
            if s[j - 1] == s[i - 1]:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1
            else:
                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])

    return dp[n][n]
```

This solution uses dynamic programming to build a table `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the longest palindromic subsequence that can be formed using characters from index `i` to `j`. The function iterates over all possible substrings of the input string and updates the `dp` table accordingly. Finally, it returns the value at `dp[n][n]`, which represents the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the entire string.

